I was in fact talling about bitmap graphics. 

It seems I haven't been entirely clear.  Text mode, IBMgraphics and all, still
works on Vista.  Both for MS-DOS and Win32 programs.

What doesn't work is _full screen_ mode.  And MS-DOS bitmap graphics require
full screen on NT-based windows.  So if you set OPTIONS=video:autodetect, the
MS-DOS NetHack will attempt to enable VGA graphics -- and Vista will immediately
kill the process.  So if you want tiles on Vista, you're pretty much stuck with
the Win32 graphical version.

If you're in text mode, with an MS-DOS or Win32 program running, and press
Alt-Enter, you get an error box and nothing else happens.

A tty-like interface actually running in a window could be resized, or run at
full screen, and also avoid a number of bugs and limitations in Windows (not all
of them new to Vista).

